{"name":"Gold Foil","hex":"#d99f4d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold foil","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thin sheet or sheets of gold that is thicker than gold leaf."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold_foil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold leaf","blocks":[{"text":"Gold leaf is gold that has been hammered into thin sheets by a process known as goldbeating, for use in gilding."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/330px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_leaf","wikidata":"Q929186"},{"title":"Foil (metal)","blocks":[{"text":"A foil is a very thin sheet of metal, typically made by hammering or rolling. Foils are most easily made with malleable metal, such as aluminium, copper, tin, and gold. Foils usually bend under their own weight and can be torn easily. For example, aluminium foil is usually about 1⁄1000 inch (0.025 mm), whereas gold can be made into foil only a few atoms thick, called gold leaf. Extremely thin foil is called metal leaf. Leaf tears very easily and must be picked up with special brushes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Aluminio.jpg/330px-Aluminio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foil_(metal)","wikidata":"Q251473"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg/500px-Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg/1280px-Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg/500px-Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Mycenaean necklace; 1400–1050 BC; gilded terracotta; diameter of the rosettes: 2.7 cm (1.1 in), with variations of about 0.1 cm (0.04 in), and a pendant length of 3.7 cm (1.5 in); Metropolitan Museum of Art (New York City)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Goldbeating_-_Weigel_%285301539%29.jpg/500px-Goldbeating_-_Weigel_%285301539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm